1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
Tanya Cushman Reviewer:" Peter van de Ven

2
00:00:15,840 --> 00:00:17,920
Tackling complexity in the heart of software

3
00:00:17,920 --> 00:00:20,360
was the subtitle of my book,

4
00:00:20,360 --> 00:00:24,920
because, to me, that was the point of the book.

5
00:00:24,920 --> 00:00:26,320
Domain-driven design, then,

6
00:00:26,320 --> 00:00:30,000
is a term to capture this particular approach,

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:32,860
the way of dealing with this,

8
00:00:32,860 --> 00:00:36,820
the way of doing this important thing.

9
00:00:36,820 --> 00:00:41,560
Of course, this presumes that you can find

10
00:00:41,560 --> 00:00:44,000
that heart of software,

11
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:48,080
which I think is the domain that we're working on,

12
00:00:48,080 --> 00:00:50,280
is where the complexity is.

13
00:00:50,280 --> 00:00:55,280
And I think that, generally, that is often true.

14
00:00:56,320 --> 00:00:58,660
But often, I'm asked, like,

15
00:00:58,660 --> 00:01:00,960
well, what exactly is domain-driven design?

16
00:01:00,960 --> 00:01:04,900
I mean, it is a hard thing to define, kind of.

17
00:01:04,900 --> 00:01:08,240
And I understand why people sometimes are concerned.

18
00:01:08,240 --> 00:01:09,960
Like, it doesn't seem like you've given us

19
00:01:09,960 --> 00:01:14,040
a really sharp definition.

20
00:01:14,040 --> 00:01:17,540
And, I mean, on the one hand, I say,

21
00:01:17,540 --> 00:01:21,320
look, there's basically these few things.

22
00:01:21,320 --> 00:01:25,120
That the point of domain-driven design, or rather,

23
00:01:25,120 --> 00:01:26,320
the...

24
00:01:26,320 --> 00:01:31,060
the principles of domain-driven design

25
00:01:31,060 --> 00:01:36,840
is that we would find that core complexity

26
00:01:36,840 --> 00:01:38,840
in a critical part of our domain,

27
00:01:38,840 --> 00:01:41,340
and we will focus on that.

28
00:01:41,340 --> 00:01:44,440
Not focus on the technical platform,

29
00:01:44,440 --> 00:01:47,340
but on the domain, and in particular,

30
00:01:47,340 --> 00:01:52,780
on high-value, difficult parts in that domain.

31
00:01:52,780 --> 00:01:55,120
And...

32
00:01:55,120 --> 00:01:56,320
then...

33
00:01:56,320 --> 00:02:00,320
in particular, how do we deal with that complexity

34
00:02:00,320 --> 00:02:05,260
in those high-value, complex parts?

35
00:02:05,260 --> 00:02:08,160
Well, we develop models of them.

36
00:02:08,160 --> 00:02:11,140
But we don't just say, okay, this is the design phase

37
00:02:11,140 --> 00:02:12,940
or whatever, and we're going to make a model,

38
00:02:12,940 --> 00:02:16,400
and it's going to be this nice, big UML diagram,

39
00:02:16,400 --> 00:02:19,840
and now it's time to program.

40
00:02:19,840 --> 00:02:23,820
It's an integral part of the whole process of development,

41
00:02:23,820 --> 00:02:25,320
and it goes on and on.

42
00:02:25,320 --> 00:02:28,860
So that's why I always say exploring models.

43
00:02:28,860 --> 00:02:32,500
Furthermore, we explore models in a...

44
00:02:32,500 --> 00:02:37,100
in a particular engagement with business people,

45
00:02:37,100 --> 00:02:38,940
or with domain experts.

46
00:02:38,940 --> 00:02:40,640
Assuming it's a business domain,

47
00:02:40,640 --> 00:02:43,180
then that would be actual business people.

48
00:02:43,180 --> 00:02:45,980
In other words, this modeling is something

49
00:02:45,980 --> 00:02:50,520
that business people and software experts do together.

50
00:02:50,520 --> 00:02:52,940
We don't say to the business people,

51
00:02:52,940 --> 00:02:54,320
you're the ones who know the business,

52
00:02:54,320 --> 00:02:56,960
so give us a model,

53
00:02:56,960 --> 00:02:59,960
which some people have proposed.

54
00:02:59,960 --> 00:03:03,660
And we don't say, well, we're the ones responsible

55
00:03:03,660 --> 00:03:06,360
for the software, so we'll develop the model,

56
00:03:06,360 --> 00:03:10,900
which is what happens by default most of the time.

57
00:03:10,900 --> 00:03:13,340
We say this is really something that if it's going to come

58
00:03:13,340 --> 00:03:18,340
out well, it needs to be done together.

59
00:03:18,340 --> 00:03:24,320
And then there's this point that the software shows

60
00:03:24,320 --> 00:03:26,960
that it should express that model.

61
00:03:26,960 --> 00:03:30,360
That a model that is just a diagram we draw on the board,

62
00:03:30,360 --> 00:03:34,560
or even a wonderful discussion we had with our business people,

63
00:03:34,560 --> 00:03:39,740
that if we come up with these nice, sharp concepts

64
00:03:39,740 --> 00:03:45,140
and so forth that guide, and then we do nothing with it,

65
00:03:45,140 --> 00:03:47,040
nothing tangible in the software,

66
00:03:47,040 --> 00:03:49,280
then it really hasn't done us much good.

67
00:03:49,280 --> 00:03:52,820
Maybe it's of some value, but in domain-driven design,

68
00:03:52,820 --> 00:03:53,980
there's this principle that if you're going to do something,

69
00:03:53,980 --> 00:03:54,020
you're going to do something wrong.

70
00:03:54,020 --> 00:03:54,080
You're going to do something wrong.

71
00:03:54,080 --> 00:03:54,240
You're going to do something wrong.

72
00:03:54,240 --> 00:03:54,280
You're going to do something wrong.

73
00:03:54,280 --> 00:03:54,320
You're going to do something wrong.

74
00:03:54,320 --> 00:03:56,880
And so we say that the software should explicitly reflect

75
00:03:56,880 --> 00:03:59,620
that model.

76
00:03:59,620 --> 00:04:05,800
And then that leads to the ubiquitous language.

77
00:04:05,800 --> 00:04:07,600
How does it reflect that model anyway?

78
00:04:07,600 --> 00:04:10,000
Well, the structures in the model,

79
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:13,140
the structures that the model suggests should show

80
00:04:13,140 --> 00:04:16,040
up in the software, of course.

81
00:04:16,040 --> 00:04:21,240
But the words, the language, a lot of domain-driven design,

82
00:04:21,240 --> 00:04:24,280
the philosophy of it revolves around how integral language,

83
00:04:24,280 --> 00:04:27,180
how integral language is to the way that we do these things.

84
00:04:27,180 --> 00:04:29,860
So that in the end, all this collaboration,

85
00:04:29,860 --> 00:04:34,020
exploration, and even the programming,

86
00:04:34,020 --> 00:04:37,220
the thread you can see running through it is the evolution

87
00:04:37,220 --> 00:04:41,640
of this language for talking about the domain problems

88
00:04:41,640 --> 00:04:45,540
in an increasingly precise and expressive way,

89
00:04:45,540 --> 00:04:50,400
to the point where you can write code that really says,

90
00:04:50,400 --> 00:04:54,040
that's like just a, it reads like an expression of,

91
00:04:54,040 --> 00:04:59,780
this is the solution we've chosen for our problem.

92
00:04:59,780 --> 00:05:03,340
Now, ubiquitous means everywhere.

93
00:05:03,340 --> 00:05:06,860
And when I say ubiquitous, then I mean when I'm talking

94
00:05:06,860 --> 00:05:08,860
to a business person, or when I'm talking

95
00:05:08,860 --> 00:05:12,260
to another technical person, or when I'm programming the computer,

96
00:05:12,260 --> 00:05:14,860
or when I'm writing a document.

97
00:05:14,860 --> 00:05:20,760
But it doesn't mean everywhere throughout the system.

98
00:05:20,760 --> 00:05:24,000
And so I have to, you know,

99
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:28,940
say ubiquitous language within a bounded context.

100
00:05:28,940 --> 00:05:33,640
A bounded context is a recognition that language

101
00:05:33,640 --> 00:05:38,220
doesn't mean anything in general.

102
00:05:38,220 --> 00:05:41,080
All the things I'm saying only make sense to you,

103
00:05:41,080 --> 00:05:43,880
if they do make any sense to you.

104
00:05:43,880 --> 00:05:46,560
But they only make sense to you because of the context

105
00:05:46,560 --> 00:05:48,520
in which I'm saying them.

106
00:05:48,520 --> 00:05:51,100
You know the things I've said before,

107
00:05:51,100 --> 00:05:54,000
you know the things in Matthias' introduction,

108
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:59,140
and other information that you bring to bear to say,

109
00:05:59,140 --> 00:06:01,780
okay, that's what this word means now.

110
00:06:01,780 --> 00:06:03,980
That's what this phrase means now.

111
00:06:03,980 --> 00:06:05,540
But it didn't just mean that.

112
00:06:05,540 --> 00:06:11,620
If you had just seen the sentence I am saying now

113
00:06:11,620 --> 00:06:15,920
on a billboard driving to this place,

114
00:06:15,920 --> 00:06:18,620
you wouldn't have known what it meant.

115
00:06:18,620 --> 00:06:22,700
So when you're reading code in a computer program,

116
00:06:22,700 --> 00:06:24,000
the same is true.

117
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:25,760
You can't just look at one line of code

118
00:06:25,760 --> 00:06:29,200
and know what it means, know what it will do.

119
00:06:29,200 --> 00:06:32,240
You have to usually read quite a bit of code

120
00:06:32,240 --> 00:06:34,380
that comes in other parts of the program.

121
00:06:34,380 --> 00:06:36,320
The code that comes before it

122
00:06:36,320 --> 00:06:39,780
in the particular process it's in.

123
00:06:39,780 --> 00:06:44,120
The code that is referenced by any kind of dependency

124
00:06:44,120 --> 00:06:46,120
in that code.

125
00:06:46,120 --> 00:06:49,120
So in order to understand a line of code,

126
00:06:49,120 --> 00:06:52,220
you have to know all these things.

127
00:06:52,220 --> 00:06:54,000
Bounded context says,

128
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:58,300
we're not going to rely on this kind of,

129
00:06:58,300 --> 00:07:00,240
well, I'll just figure it out as I go along.

130
00:07:00,240 --> 00:07:03,500
I'll look up, you know, every line of code

131
00:07:03,500 --> 00:07:05,480
and then every reference it makes.

132
00:07:05,480 --> 00:07:07,120
And then by the time I get to that line,

133
00:07:07,120 --> 00:07:09,780
I'll know what it means, what it will do.

134
00:07:09,780 --> 00:07:13,760
We're saying, we will define some concepts.

135
00:07:13,760 --> 00:07:17,960
And within a boundary that we have specified,

136
00:07:17,960 --> 00:07:22,660
this will be a very consistent meaning.

137
00:07:22,660 --> 00:07:23,800
And that we will have, you know,

138
00:07:23,800 --> 00:07:25,900
we will have some rules.

139
00:07:25,900 --> 00:07:28,040
And within that boundary,

140
00:07:28,040 --> 00:07:33,040
those rules will apply very consistently and rigorously.

141
00:07:33,040 --> 00:07:34,240
And you say, well, of course,

142
00:07:34,240 --> 00:07:36,980
that's just what modeling is all about,

143
00:07:36,980 --> 00:07:39,420
what good design is all about.

144
00:07:39,420 --> 00:07:42,420
But the thing is, without the boundary,

145
00:07:42,420 --> 00:07:44,960
it doesn't work, it doesn't even make sense.

146
00:07:46,120 --> 00:07:49,160
If I, you know, to take an extremely simple example,

147
00:07:49,160 --> 00:07:51,420
if I said there's a number x and a number y,

148
00:07:51,420 --> 00:07:53,760
and x will always be less than y,

149
00:07:53,800 --> 00:07:58,800
and now you see some calculation in my program,

150
00:08:01,480 --> 00:08:03,680
and it says y minus x,

151
00:08:03,680 --> 00:08:07,380
then you know that that will be a positive number,

152
00:08:07,380 --> 00:08:09,280
because x is always less than y.

153
00:08:09,280 --> 00:08:10,120
I said so.

154
00:08:11,580 --> 00:08:16,160
And now you go and you look at another computer program,

155
00:08:16,160 --> 00:08:18,500
and you see y minus x.

156
00:08:18,500 --> 00:08:21,620
You see that actual text somewhere in the program.

157
00:08:21,620 --> 00:08:23,060
Is it a positive number?

158
00:08:23,800 --> 00:08:28,800
Maybe, maybe not, maybe it's not even a number.

159
00:08:28,800 --> 00:08:31,940
You simply do not know, and you cannot know,

160
00:08:31,940 --> 00:08:36,440
there is no meaning to expressions

161
00:08:36,440 --> 00:08:38,540
outside of a given context.

162
00:08:38,540 --> 00:08:42,480
So domain-driven design tries to acknowledge that

163
00:08:42,480 --> 00:08:44,760
and use that to our benefit.

164
00:08:44,760 --> 00:08:46,360
Use it in several ways.

165
00:08:46,360 --> 00:08:48,420
One of which is simply to say,

166
00:08:48,420 --> 00:08:52,060
if we really want this to work, then we need that boundary.

167
00:08:52,060 --> 00:08:53,420
I need to be able to draw a boundary around that boundary,

168
00:08:53,420 --> 00:08:55,920
a boundary around me so that I can do all the cool stuff

169
00:08:55,920 --> 00:08:58,960
that I want to do inside that boundary.

170
00:08:58,960 --> 00:09:00,600
The boundary has to be tangible.

171
00:09:00,600 --> 00:09:02,360
It's a real piece of software,

172
00:09:02,360 --> 00:09:05,060
or markers within the software.

173
00:09:07,260 --> 00:09:11,440
And that's the first and most basic way

174
00:09:11,440 --> 00:09:14,200
in which it's important to domain-driven design.

175
00:09:15,480 --> 00:09:18,540
Another way it's important to domain-driven design

176
00:09:18,540 --> 00:09:23,420
is to say we aren't going to have just one model,

177
00:09:23,420 --> 00:09:25,960
but probably several models.

178
00:09:25,960 --> 00:09:29,920
Because we have a big, sprawling domain

179
00:09:29,920 --> 00:09:31,600
that has many problems.

180
00:09:31,600 --> 00:09:34,460
Multiple, complex, important problems,

181
00:09:34,460 --> 00:09:37,960
and we want to address them differently.

182
00:09:37,960 --> 00:09:42,640
So just as we have many tools for every kind of work we do,

183
00:09:42,640 --> 00:09:48,880
we have many models to address a specific domain.

184
00:09:48,880 --> 00:09:53,280
A third way in which it's important is that most projects

185
00:09:53,280 --> 00:09:56,520
of any size have multiple teams.

186
00:09:56,520 --> 00:10:02,260
And different teams cannot coordinate as tightly around,

187
00:10:02,260 --> 00:10:05,760
I mean, to accomplish the kind of clarity and consistency

188
00:10:05,760 --> 00:10:07,800
that I was talking about earlier,

189
00:10:07,800 --> 00:10:11,760
you need a very well-coordinated team to do that.

190
00:10:11,760 --> 00:10:15,740
Two teams, each can coordinate within themselves,

191
00:10:15,740 --> 00:10:19,240
but they can't coordinate as well with each other.

192
00:10:19,240 --> 00:10:22,200
And so they can't accomplish that kind of consistency

193
00:10:22,200 --> 00:10:23,280
between them.

194
00:10:23,280 --> 00:10:25,820
They can in one particular way.

195
00:10:25,820 --> 00:10:29,280
They can do it by freezing the model in place,

196
00:10:29,280 --> 00:10:31,320
by deciding in advance what the rules are

197
00:10:31,320 --> 00:10:33,360
and then not breaking them.

198
00:10:33,360 --> 00:10:38,420
But if you want to be able to make strict rules

199
00:10:38,420 --> 00:10:41,640
and then adjust and evolve them quickly

200
00:10:41,640 --> 00:10:44,060
in response to new insights,

201
00:10:44,060 --> 00:10:47,800
then you need to be able to do that within a team.

202
00:10:47,800 --> 00:10:51,300
So the fact that we usually have multiple teams working

203
00:10:51,300 --> 00:10:52,640
is another reason that we need

204
00:10:52,640 --> 00:10:55,680
to have a very well-coordinated context.

205
00:10:55,680 --> 00:10:58,680
Anyway, so that's basically the gist

206
00:10:58,680 --> 00:11:01,040
of domain-driven design.

207
00:11:01,040 --> 00:11:10,120
And now I know that even so, I think what I said probably

208
00:11:10,120 --> 00:11:13,420
doesn't sound like a very, I don't know,

209
00:11:13,420 --> 00:11:18,460
it's not a reductionist kind of an attitude, I think.

210
00:11:18,460 --> 00:11:21,660
You know, I think I've given up on trying to have

211
00:11:21,660 --> 00:11:22,640
that very, very, very, very, very, very, very, very,

212
00:11:22,640 --> 00:11:26,640
very crisp little definition of domain-driven design.

213
00:11:26,640 --> 00:11:31,380
It's kind of a, it's kind of a gestalt

214
00:11:31,380 --> 00:11:35,720
that after a while, it's sort of a way

215
00:11:35,720 --> 00:11:38,280
that you look at every problem.

216
00:11:39,660 --> 00:11:41,320
No, not every problem, of course,

217
00:11:41,320 --> 00:11:44,200
but the sorts of problems they address as well,

218
00:11:44,200 --> 00:11:48,140
like complex domains where you want to write software.

219
00:11:49,440 --> 00:11:50,640
And it gives some guidance.

220
00:11:50,640 --> 00:11:51,600
And then, of course, there are

221
00:11:51,600 --> 00:11:52,640
some concrete techniques.

222
00:11:52,640 --> 00:11:54,100
There are some techniques that we tend to use,

223
00:11:54,100 --> 00:11:57,680
but those are more flexible, more fluid.

224
00:11:57,680 --> 00:12:00,040
At this conference, you'll see people presenting

225
00:12:00,040 --> 00:12:04,720
about different ways of doing some of those things.

226
00:12:07,020 --> 00:12:12,020
And so, as I say, I don't know if I can really define it

227
00:12:14,420 --> 00:12:16,400
in that sort of way.

228
00:12:17,840 --> 00:12:19,840
And, of course, one important thing about it

229
00:12:19,840 --> 00:12:22,640
is it gives us some terminology, some ways of talking,

230
00:12:22,640 --> 00:12:25,000
about some of the important things we're doing,

231
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:27,880
even if we don't exactly agree on the technique

232
00:12:27,880 --> 00:12:29,280
that we're applying to it.

233
00:12:29,280 --> 00:12:33,920
So, for example, to have the word bounded context

234
00:12:33,920 --> 00:12:38,960
to describe and say, I think that the boundary

235
00:12:38,960 --> 00:12:41,160
of this context has some problems,

236
00:12:41,160 --> 00:12:44,400
and that actually means something to the other person,

237
00:12:44,400 --> 00:12:46,100
that's one of the valuable things.

238
00:12:46,100 --> 00:12:51,100
Now, why is it that 12 years after my book was published,

239
00:12:57,920 --> 00:13:02,380
we're actually still talking about domain-driven design?

240
00:13:02,380 --> 00:13:03,860
And not only still talking about it,

241
00:13:03,860 --> 00:13:08,320
but actually, in some ways, amping up the conversation,

242
00:13:08,320 --> 00:13:09,580
because this is, as you said,

243
00:13:09,580 --> 00:13:13,160
the first real conference of our own.

244
00:13:14,200 --> 00:13:15,520
And how is that?

245
00:13:15,520 --> 00:13:16,100
I don't know.

246
00:13:16,100 --> 00:13:21,100
I mean, it's kind of a surprise to me, truly.

247
00:13:21,640 --> 00:13:23,980
But, I mean, a very pleasant one.

248
00:13:25,600 --> 00:13:29,920
But I think one reason, one possibility, anyway,

249
00:13:29,920 --> 00:13:31,440
involves technology.

250
00:13:32,580 --> 00:13:34,880
And that's kind of a funny thing to say

251
00:13:34,880 --> 00:13:38,120
after making the point that the first point

252
00:13:38,120 --> 00:13:41,100
of domain-driven design was to focus

253
00:13:41,100 --> 00:13:45,360
on the core complexity in the heart of the domain.

254
00:13:46,100 --> 00:13:47,500
And so on.

255
00:13:47,500 --> 00:13:50,040
And that we emphasize those domain problems

256
00:13:50,040 --> 00:13:54,640
and not the technical ones, and not the technology, and so on.

257
00:13:54,640 --> 00:13:57,920
And it's true that domain-driven design isn't about technology.

258
00:13:57,920 --> 00:14:01,020
And in fact, I mean, part of what made me write it

259
00:14:01,020 --> 00:14:04,620
was a bit of a response to what I saw around me,

260
00:14:04,620 --> 00:14:10,500
that all the best software designers were always focused

261
00:14:10,500 --> 00:14:14,120
toward the technical aspects of the software.

262
