00:46:37,640 --> 00:46:39,640
A lot of, you know, okay.

734
00:46:39,640 --> 00:46:43,640
There we go.

735
00:46:43,640 --> 00:46:47,640
It's easy to find something that's not useful for anything.

736
00:46:47,640 --> 00:46:50,640
Actually, this might be useful for one thing,

737
00:46:50,640 --> 00:46:54,640
which is I need to have a kind of generic schema

738
00:46:54,640 --> 00:46:57,640
that I can just shove all our data into

739
00:46:57,640 --> 00:47:01,640
as a kind of a repository of some stuff

740
00:47:01,640 --> 00:47:03,640
that we might look up from time to time.

741
00:47:03,640 --> 00:47:05,640
But if you're actually trying to do the things

742
00:47:05,640 --> 00:47:07,640
that domain-driven design is focused on,

743
00:47:07,640 --> 00:47:09,640
this is not useful.

744
00:47:09,640 --> 00:47:11,640
Well, as I was saying,

745
00:47:11,640 --> 00:47:14,640
and I want to wrap up with a little optimism,

746
00:47:14,640 --> 00:47:17,640
because sometimes I don't sound very optimistic

747
00:47:17,640 --> 00:47:21,640
because I talk about how not all of a large system

748
00:47:21,640 --> 00:47:23,640
will be well designed.

749
00:47:23,640 --> 00:47:26,640
And I talk about all the dysfunction in a large organization

750
00:47:26,640 --> 00:47:29,640
that is going to make sure that is always true.

751
00:47:29,640 --> 00:47:32,640
And the intrinsic difficulty of software development

752
00:47:32,640 --> 00:47:35,640
that will ensure that it's always true anyway.

753
00:47:35,640 --> 00:47:37,640
And so people say, oh, this just sounds,

754
00:47:37,640 --> 00:47:39,640
this makes me feel bad.

755
00:47:39,640 --> 00:47:41,640
Well, I don't mean it that way.

756
00:47:41,640 --> 00:47:43,640
I mean it like, let's be realistic.

757
00:47:43,640 --> 00:47:45,640
Not all of the software will be well designed,

758
00:47:45,640 --> 00:47:49,640
and so we'd better focus, create a boundary,

759
00:47:49,640 --> 00:47:52,640
and make one piece that's well designed,

760
00:47:52,640 --> 00:47:55,640
an important piece.

761
00:47:55,640 --> 00:47:57,640
But optimism.

762
00:47:57,640 --> 00:48:00,640
Because I think there's reason to think

763
00:48:00,640 --> 00:48:02,640
that the present,

764
00:48:02,640 --> 00:48:04,640
and the future,

765
00:48:04,640 --> 00:48:07,640
are going to be better than the recent past.

766
00:48:07,640 --> 00:48:10,640
In a way, you could say that we have,

767
00:48:10,640 --> 00:48:12,640
at this point,

768
00:48:12,640 --> 00:48:15,640
at least three, maybe more,

769
00:48:15,640 --> 00:48:17,640
actual modeling paradigms.

770
00:48:17,640 --> 00:48:19,640
As I said, in 2003,

771
00:48:19,640 --> 00:48:21,640
everything was object-oriented.

772
00:48:21,640 --> 00:48:23,640
If you wanted to do,

773
00:48:23,640 --> 00:48:27,640
you know, if you wanted to use models in software,

774
00:48:27,640 --> 00:48:30,640
object-oriented was the first place,

775
00:48:30,640 --> 00:48:32,640
the first paradigm,

776
00:48:32,640 --> 00:48:34,640
the first time of programming

777
00:48:34,640 --> 00:48:37,640
that took that concept seriously.

778
00:48:37,640 --> 00:48:40,640
But we have more now.

779
00:48:40,640 --> 00:48:41,640
I mean, and by the way,

780
00:48:41,640 --> 00:48:43,640
I love object-oriented programming,

781
00:48:43,640 --> 00:48:45,640
and I know it's a little out of vogue right now,

782
00:48:45,640 --> 00:48:48,640
and I actually find that quite refreshing.

783
00:48:48,640 --> 00:48:51,640
I'm enjoying doing things in different ways.

784
00:48:51,640 --> 00:48:57,640
In fact, but this is one of the options we have now.

785
00:48:57,640 --> 00:48:59,640
Event sourcing, I would say,

786
00:48:59,640 --> 00:49:01,640
after thinking about it for a few years,

787
00:49:01,640 --> 00:49:03,640
I realized that it's essentially

788
00:49:03,640 --> 00:49:05,640
a different modeling paradigm.

789
00:49:05,640 --> 00:49:08,640
It says, you know, that you'll represent things

790
00:49:08,640 --> 00:49:10,640
primarily as a sequence of events,

791
00:49:10,640 --> 00:49:14,640
and then you will infer,

792
00:49:14,640 --> 00:49:18,640
or you will project from those events

793
00:49:18,640 --> 00:49:20,640
to create a view of whatever it is

794
00:49:20,640 --> 00:49:22,640
that you're interested in.

795
00:49:22,640 --> 00:49:25,640
And it eliminates mutation of data,

796
00:49:25,640 --> 00:49:28,640
and it can be very expressive

797
00:49:28,640 --> 00:49:30,640
for certain kinds of problems.

798
00:49:30,640 --> 00:49:33,640
But your model takes a different shape.

799
00:49:33,640 --> 00:49:34,640
It's not like, well, okay,

800
00:49:34,640 --> 00:49:35,640
let's figure out what the model is,

801
00:49:35,640 --> 00:49:37,640
and now we're going to program it

802
00:49:37,640 --> 00:49:40,640
in whatever language that we choose independently.

803
00:49:40,640 --> 00:49:42,640
No, the two shape each other.

804
00:49:42,640 --> 00:49:44,640
You have to create a model

805
00:49:44,640 --> 00:49:46,640
that fits the paradigm

806
00:49:46,640 --> 00:49:50,640
that you're going to use to build it.

807
00:49:50,640 --> 00:49:52,640
Relational.

808
00:49:52,640 --> 00:49:54,640
I think relational is a genuine modeling paradigm.

809
00:49:54,640 --> 00:49:56,640
It was intended to be that.

810
00:49:56,640 --> 00:49:59,640
They had, you know, ER models

811
00:49:59,640 --> 00:50:01,640
and...

812
00:50:01,640 --> 00:50:03,640
But we stopped using it for that, basically,

813
00:50:03,640 --> 00:50:06,640
because it became, for a decade or more,

814
00:50:06,640 --> 00:50:09,640
just a bad way to store objects.

815
00:50:09,640 --> 00:50:11,640
And so people have practically forgotten

816
00:50:11,640 --> 00:50:14,640
what a really powerful tool it is.

817
00:50:14,640 --> 00:50:16,640
If you actually say,

818
00:50:16,640 --> 00:50:18,640
hey, this is a problem where we have to,

819
00:50:18,640 --> 00:50:23,640
you know, compare and manipulate big sets,

820
00:50:23,640 --> 00:50:25,640
I'm going to actually just express that

821
00:50:25,640 --> 00:50:28,640
in terms of SQL queries,

822
00:50:28,640 --> 00:50:32,640
and I will create a schema

823
00:50:32,640 --> 00:50:34,640
that is actually tailored

824
00:50:34,640 --> 00:50:38,640
to the problems I'm trying to compute about.

825
00:50:38,640 --> 00:50:40,640
That's not the kind of schema people have made

826
00:50:40,640 --> 00:50:42,640
for the last 20 years, in my experience.

827
00:50:42,640 --> 00:50:44,640
They create schemas that are meant to be

828
00:50:44,640 --> 00:50:46,640
sort of generalized places

829
00:50:46,640 --> 00:50:49,640
to put all of our data.

830
00:50:49,640 --> 00:50:51,640
That's not what I mean by, you know, a model.

831
00:50:51,640 --> 00:50:53,640
And that's not what I mean when I say

832
00:50:53,640 --> 00:50:55,640
relational is a paradigm that you can use

833
00:50:55,640 --> 00:50:57,640
for domain-driven design.

834
00:50:57,640 --> 00:50:58,640
What I mean, though, if you say,

835
00:50:58,640 --> 00:51:00,640
okay, I'm not going to use that schema,

836
00:51:00,640 --> 00:51:01,640
it's fine for that one to exist,

837
00:51:01,640 --> 00:51:03,640
but I'm not using it for this.

838
00:51:03,640 --> 00:51:06,640
What I'm going to do is create a schema

839
00:51:06,640 --> 00:51:10,640
where we take just the information we want to apply

840
00:51:10,640 --> 00:51:13,640
and structure it in a certain way

841
00:51:13,640 --> 00:51:16,640
that allows us to do, you know,

842
00:51:16,640 --> 00:51:19,640
set operations, basically, to find our answer.

843
00:51:19,640 --> 00:51:25,640
So in that sense, I think it's a modeling paradigm.

844
00:51:25,640 --> 00:51:26,640
And then functional.

845
00:51:26,640 --> 00:51:30,640
Now, you know, functional is a big thing,

846
00:51:30,640 --> 00:51:32,640
maybe more than one thing.

847
00:51:32,640 --> 00:51:34,640
And so there are people, though,

848
00:51:34,640 --> 00:51:37,640
who have been talking about modeling

849
00:51:37,640 --> 00:51:40,640
in the functional realm

850
00:51:40,640 --> 00:51:43,640
and doing very interesting things.

851
00:51:43,640 --> 00:51:48,640
The thing is, models are just systems of abstraction.

852
00:51:48,640 --> 00:51:52,640
And so if you have a powerful mechanism for abstraction,

853
00:51:52,640 --> 00:51:56,640
you should be able to implement

854
00:51:56,640 --> 00:51:59,640
and you should be able to express models.

855
00:51:59,640 --> 00:52:02,640
Furthermore, if you want to, you know,

856
00:52:02,640 --> 00:52:06,640
bring that ubiquitous language to life in the code,

857
00:52:06,640 --> 00:52:08,640
well, some of the functional languages, I think,

858
00:52:08,640 --> 00:52:14,640
are really nice for making language in the code.

859
00:52:14,640 --> 00:52:17,640
And it might be a good mate with event sourcing.

860
00:52:17,640 --> 00:52:19,640
I mean, I'm just sort of laying out, like,

861
00:52:19,640 --> 00:52:22,640
I'm pointing out that we have so many options,

862
00:52:22,640 --> 00:52:25,640
options that were really not there

863
00:52:25,640 --> 00:52:28,640
in 2003.

864
00:52:28,640 --> 00:52:30,640
And graphs, graph databases,

865
00:52:30,640 --> 00:52:33,640
it's more than just a way to store data,

866
00:52:33,640 --> 00:52:35,640
store highly connected data.

867
00:52:35,640 --> 00:52:36,640
That's the way they describe it.

868
00:52:36,640 --> 00:52:38,640
That's not what I think about when I look at it.

869
00:52:38,640 --> 00:52:42,640
I think this is a way to model certain kinds of problems,

870
00:52:42,640 --> 00:52:44,640
you know.

871
00:52:44,640 --> 00:52:48,640
And it's brilliantly clear.

872
00:52:48,640 --> 00:52:51,640
You know, this is one of those classic kind of modeling paradigms

873
00:52:51,640 --> 00:52:53,640
that goes back long before computers.

874
00:52:53,640 --> 00:52:54,640
So...

875
00:52:55,640 --> 00:52:58,640
Anyway...

876
00:52:58,640 --> 00:53:02,640
And maybe this one will come along, but...

877
00:53:02,640 --> 00:53:05,640
So, in a nutshell, I would say that

878
00:53:05,640 --> 00:53:09,640
maybe it's a good time to be

879
00:53:09,640 --> 00:53:13,640
taking DDD to the next level a little bit.

880
00:53:13,640 --> 00:53:15,640
Maybe our tools are ready.

881
00:53:15,640 --> 00:53:20,640
Maybe there are things, too, that are pushing us toward...

882
00:53:20,640 --> 00:53:24,640
toward more concern about design.

883
00:53:24,640 --> 00:53:27,640
If you look at the typical, you know,

884
00:53:27,640 --> 00:53:32,640
cloud-based application or mobile,

885
00:53:32,640 --> 00:53:35,640
you know, tying all these things together,

886
00:53:35,640 --> 00:53:38,640
it becomes perhaps a bit more...

887
00:53:38,640 --> 00:53:42,640
That is, you can't get away with some of the sloppiness

888
00:53:42,640 --> 00:53:44,640
that you can get away with

889
00:53:44,640 --> 00:53:47,640
if everything's together on one server.

890
00:53:47,640 --> 00:53:52,640
And the fact that you will not be forgiven for sloppiness

